    "examBoard": "Federal Board",
    "keyPoints": [
      "Lipids defined as water-insoluble organic compounds with functions in energy storage, signaling, and cell membranes",
      "Essential lipids cannot be synthesized by body (e.g., omega-3, omega-6 fatty acids); non-essential lipids can be synthesized internally",
      "Hydrolysis reaction: triglyceride + water → glycerol + 3 fatty acids (reversible, acid/enzyme catalyzed)",
      "Saponification reaction: triglyceride + strong base → glycerol + 3 fatty acid salts/soap (irreversible)",
      "Saponification produces amphipathic molecules used as detergents in soap manufacturing"
    ],
    "lastAttempt": null,
    "locked": false,
    "marks": 6,
    "modelAnswer": "Lipids are a heterogeneous group of naturally occurring organic compounds found in animals and plants, characterized by their solubility in organic solvents and insolubility in water. They serve as energy-storing molecules, chemical messengers, cell membrane constituents, and food storage molecules.\n\n**Essential vs. Non-Essential Lipids:**\nEssential lipids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from dietary sources, such as certain polyunsaturated fatty acids (omega-3 and omega-6). Non-essential lipids can be synthesized by the body from other precursors, such as cholesterol and saturated fatty acids.\n\n**Reactions of a General Triglyceride:**\n\n**a. Hydrolysis:**\nTriglycerides undergo hydrolysis in the presence of water and an acid or enzyme catalyst, breaking the ester bonds to produce glycerol and three fatty acids:\n$$\\text{C}_{3}\\text{H}_{5}(\\text{OCOR})_{3} + 3\\text{H}_{2}\\text{O} \\xrightarrow{\\text{H}^{+} \\text{ or enzyme}} \\text{C}_{3}\\text{H}_{5}(\\text{OH})_{3} + 3\\text{RCOOH}$$\nThis reaction is reversible and occurs during fat digestion in the small intestine.\n\n**b. Saponification:**\nSaponification is the hydrolysis of triglycerides in the presence of a strong base (typically NaOH or KOH), producing glycerol and fatty acid salts (soap):\n$$\\text{C}_{3}\\text{H}_{5}(\\text{OCOR})_{3} + 3\\text{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\text{C}_{3}\\text{H}_{5}(\\text{OH})_{3} + 3\\text{RCOONa}$$\nThis is an irreversible reaction used industrially in soap manufacturing. The fatty acid salts produced are amphipathic molecules with hydrophobic tails and hydrophilic heads, enabling them to act as detergents.",
    "partType": "C",
    "questionKind": "long",
    "questionNumber": "6",
    "session": "annual",
    "stem": "Describe lipids. How can essential and non-essential lipids be differentiated? Write down the following reactions of a general triglyceride: a. Hydrolysis b. Saponification",
    "topicId": "ms7c4x1hc0rha1fys4x85zf88d85pq76",
    "year": 2024
  },
